  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the sum of the measures of the six minor arcs on the circumcircle of triangle \\( \\triangle DEF \\), given that the points \\( G, D, E, H, J, \\) and \\( F \\) divide the circumcircle into these arcs.\n\nFirst, we need to understand the properties of the triangle and its circumcircle. The angles of triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\) are given as \\( \\angle BAC = 84^\\circ \\), \\( \\angle ABC = 60^\\circ \\), and \\( \\angle ACB = 36^\\circ \\). The midpoints \\( D, E, \\) and \\( F \\) divide the sides \\( BC, AC, \\) and \\( AB \\) respectively into equal segments.\n\nThe circumcircle of \\( \\triangle DEF \\) is a circle that passes through the midpoints of the sides of \\( \\triangle ABC \\). This circle is known as the nine-point circle of \\( \\triangle ABC \\).\n\nThe points \\( G, D, E, H, J, \\) and \\( F \\) are points on the circumcircle of \\( \\triangle DEF \\). The angles subtended by the arcs \\( \\widehat{DE}, \\widehat{HJ}, \\) and \\( \\widehat{FG} \\) at the center of the circumcircle of \\( \\triangle DEF \\) are related to the angles of triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\).\n\nWe need to find the measures of the arcs \\( \\widehat{DE}, \\widehat{HJ}, \\) and \\( \\widehat{FG} \\). The sum of the measures of all six arcs on the circumcircle of \\( \\triangle DEF \\) is \\( 360^\\circ \\). Since the points \\( G, D, E, H, J, \\) and \\( F \\) divide the circumcircle into these arcs, we need to find the sum of the measures of the arcs \\( \\widehat{DE}, \\widehat{HJ}, \\) and \\( \\widehat{FG} \\).\n\nThe sum of the measures of the arcs \\( \\widehat{DE}, \\widehat{HJ}, \\) and \\( \\widehat{FG} \\) is given by:\n\\[ \\widehat{DE} + 2 \\cdot \\widehat{HJ} + 3 \\cdot \\widehat{FG} \\]\n\nSince the sum of the measures of all six arcs is \\( 360^\\circ \\), we can write:\n\\[ \\widehat{DE} + \\widehat{HJ} + \\widehat{FG} + 2 \\cdot \\widehat{HJ} + 3 \\cdot \\widehat{FG} = 360^\\circ \\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[ \\widehat{DE} + 3 \\cdot \\widehat{HJ} + 4 \\cdot \\widehat{FG} = 360^\\circ \\]\n\nSince the sum of the measures of the arcs \\( \\widehat{DE}, \\widehat{HJ}, \\) and \\( \\widehat{FG} \\) is \\( 180^\\circ \\) (because the points \\( G, D, E, H, J, \\) and \\( F \\) are points on the circumcircle of \\( \\triangle DEF \\)), we can write:\n\\[ \\widehat{DE} + \\widehat{HJ} + \\widehat{FG} = 180^\\circ \\]\n\nSubstituting this into the previous equation, we get:\n\\[ 180^\\circ + 2 \\cdot \\widehat{HJ} + 3 \\cdot \\widehat{FG} = 360^\\circ \\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[ 2 \\cdot \\widehat{HJ} + 3 \\cdot \\widehat{FG} = 180^\\circ \\]\n\nSince the sum of the measures of the arcs \\( \\widehat{DE}, \\widehat{HJ}, \\) and \\( \\widehat{FG} \\) is \\( 180^\\circ \\), we can write:\n\\[ \\widehat{DE} + 2 \\cdot \\widehat{HJ} + 3 \\cdot \\widehat{FG} = 180^\\circ \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{180^\\circ} \\]",
